## Context
Monolith's apps startup process is a very heavy one, a lot of things happen beyond the usual aspnet app setup (DI registrations and request pipeline setup). For example, we build build our Global SiteSettings object (load from db, merge values, etc), we hookup our custom events, we reload Staff accounts, we init PRIZM events library, create redis connections, etc. 

This heavy startup process has a big impact in two scenarios: Deploying the monolith apps and Local DevX.

#### Deploying the monolith
This heavy startup process, make deployment slow, but as also some of the operations performed are CPU intensive, it generates big spikes of CPU usage on the node hosting the app. With the migration of SOE customers to the new cell, these CPU spikes became a huge problem. 

With the new SOE's cell architecture, when we deploy a new version, we do it to a whole tier (dev/uat/prod), we deploy to multiple instances on the same cluster at once. When multiple pods of the same service type (i.e. StackOverflow pods) get assigned to the same node and all start at the same time, that CPU spike gets multiplied taking the node's CPU usage to very high levels. Although we can increase the pod's requests, this spike only happens at startup, so increasing the requests means reserving node capacity through out the app's lifetime that won't be used.

For this CPU spikes, we are primarily interested in the CPU intensive operations. During startup there are some slow operations related to querying a DB or connecting to an external service (i.e. Redis or ElasticSearch). Although these add up to the startup time, they are async and not using the node's CPU, so we are not focusing on those now. 

#### Local DevX
During local development we are constantly making changes to the code and loading the local version of the app to see the changes in action. Each time that we make a change, we need to compile and the app restarts because the assemblies changed. So, each time that we load the local app after a change, we pay have to wait for this slow startup time. 

## Reflection is expensive
Reflection is slow and CPU intensive, and **we use lots of it**. Doing some profiling in startup, we identified that the `Events.Hookup` operation was taking several seconds in SOE. Why was that?

We use so much reflection, that loading all the types on the assembly each time would be expensive, so we have a property called `Env.TypesInStackOverflowAssembliesInAppDomain` which returns all the types present in the StackOverflow assemblies that are part of the running app ([code](https://github.com/StackEng/StackOverflow/blob/060273b90a892d80244ae764e3631960881dfd99/StackOverflow.Common/Env.cs#L665)). This property caches the types, however the first usage pays the price, and it is a BIG one. 

In SOE, loading all these types via reflection takes over 3 seconds:

<img width="858" height="345" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d50ebc7d-69bf-46a4-a4c7-494f29dd91e1" />

## Source generators: Reflection's fast replacement
What if instead of discovering each of the types on each StackOverflow assembly each assembly already came with a TypeRegistry with all its types? We can build that with a **source generator**!

This is what this PR is all about, the new project called `StackOverflow.TypeRegistryGenerator`. 

This project actually contains 2 generators: `TypeRegistryGenerator` and `TypeRegistryAggregatorGenerator`.

#### `TypeRegistryGenerator`
This generator will create a pipeline from Roselyn's `SyntaxProvider`. It will first identify every type declaration that we can include, excluding private and internal sub classes, obsolete ones, etc. Then it'll create a `TypeRegistry` class with a `GetTypes` method that just returns a list of hardcoded types by doing a `typeof` of each of the identified types. 

The result is something like this:

```c#
// <auto-generated/>
#pragma warning disable CS0612 // Type or member is obsolete
#pragma warning disable CS0618 // Type or member is obsolete
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Collections.Immutable;

namespace StackOverflow.Generated
{
   public static class TypeRegistry
    {
        private static ImmutableArray<Type> _types;

        public static ImmutableArray<Type> Types
        {
            get
            {
                if(_types == null)
                {
                   var builder = ImmutableArray.CreateBuilder<Type>(1683);
                   builder.Add(typeof(StackOverflow.Controllers.AdminController.ModeratorMenuDeleteOrDestroyReason));
                   builder.Add(typeof(StackOverflow.Controllers.AdminController.ModeratorMenuDeleteOrDestroyReason.DeleteReasonSubtype));
                   builder.Add(typeof(StackOverflow.Controllers.AdminController.ModeratorMenuModel));
                   builder.Add(typeof(StackOverflow.Controllers.AdminController.ModeratorMenuModel.Screen));
                   builder.Add(typeof(StackOverflow.Controllers.AdminController.PostReviewHistoryModel));
                    _types = builder.ToImmutableArray();
                }
                return _types;
            }
        }
    }
}
#pragma warning restore CS0612 // Type or member is obsolete
#pragma warning restore CS0618 // Type or member is obsolete

```

#### `TypeRegistryAggregatorGenerator`
One limitation of source generators is that they run on a specific project and they can only get the syntax symbols and generate code for that project. However, we need a single list of all the types of StackOverflow assemblies in order to replace the current `Env.TypesInStackOverflowAssembliesInAppDomain` reflection based implementation. We need something that aggregates the `TypeRegistry` classes that are being generated in each assembly. 

That's the purpose of `TypeRegistryAggregatorGenerator`. Although generators can't access to the syntax symbols of referenced projects, they can access to the already compiled references and their types. So, this generator identifies from the project's referenced assemblies, which of them have a `TypeRegistry` and then creates a new class that calls the `GetTypes() method` in each of them and aggregates them.

The result is something like this:

```c#
// <auto-generated/>
// Aggregating 5 type registries from assemblies that reference the generator
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Collections.Immutable;
using System.Linq;

namespace StackOverflow.Generated
{
    internal static class StackOverflowTypes
    {
        private static ImmutableArray<Type> _allTypes;

        public static ImmutableArray<Type> AllTypes
        {
            get
            {
                if (_allTypes == null)
                {
                    var builder = ImmutableArray.CreateBuilder<Type>(5);
               builder.AddRange(StackOverflow.Generated.TypeRegistry.Types);
               builder.AddRange(StackOverflow_ChannelsDbApi_Common.Generated.TypeRegistry.Types);
               builder.AddRange(StackOverflow_Common.Generated.TypeRegistry.Types);
               builder.AddRange(StackOverflow_Common_AspNet.Generated.TypeRegistry.Types);
               builder.AddRange(StackOverflow_Common_Models.Generated.TypeRegistry.Types);
                   _allTypes = builder.ToImmutable();
                }
                return _allTypes;
            }
        }

    }
}

```

## How to integrate it
Each class that adds the `StackOverflow.TypeRegistryGenerator` reference will have a `StackOverflowTypes` class in the assembly, however, we'll only use this aggregator class in the main projects (StackOverflow, Apiv2, Apiv3). In the startup of the main projects we need to do:

```
Env.InitializeStackOverflowTypes(Generated.StackOverflowTypes.AllTypes.ToImmutableArray());
```

If we don't call `Env.InitializeStackOverflowTypes`, and call `Env.TypesInStackOverflowAssembliesInAppDomain`, it will still work. However, it will use a "slow" initialization via reflection. It will look for all the `TypeRegistry` classes in the AppDomain assemblies and call the `Types` property to get all the types and aggregate them.  This is useful for example when running tests, where we might not want to call `Env.InitializeStackOverflowTypes` many times. 

## Results
With this change, in SOE we see a 14x improvement in `Env.TypesInStackOverflowAssembliesInAppDomain` which happens during startup.

